WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.230
Kelly, see Pete and I live in the same building.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.150
So we decided to meet at 8 a.m.

3
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.030
8 a.m. to come to work together.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.550
I had waited 15 minutes when Pete arrived.

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.250
After Pete had wasted time waiting for coffee, we were late.

6
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.030
I left you a message.

7
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.130
I didn't get that message.

8
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.250
Oh, sorry.

9
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:37.710
Pete?

10
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.550
Anna left a message.

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.990
That's the right thing to do.

12
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.250
Did you find some examples of the past perfect tense?

13
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.110
I did.

14
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.170
Anna said.

15
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.250
After Pete had wasted time waiting for coffee, we were late.

16
00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.130
Look at that coffee.

17
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.130
It looks more like dessert.

18
00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:59.090
Okay.

19
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.190
Deep watching for past perfect.

20
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.290
Yeah, that's not why we're late.

21
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.230
This is why we're late.

22
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.850
I had arrived on time at 8 a.m.

23
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.710
but didn't see Anna.

24
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.990
She was standing behind a tree.

25
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.510
I think she was hugging it.

26
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.650
I always walk to work,

27
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.350
but she said that would take too long

28
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.870
and that a scooter would be faster.

29
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.910
It was awful.

30
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.110
I hated it and it added too much time to our commute.

31
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.470
Then Anna stopped by a pond to feed the birds.

32
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.170
She had named them after characters from books

33
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.410
and yelled the names out loud.

34
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.590
Romeo, Juliet, Sherlock.

35
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.870
By the time she had fed all the birds, we were late.

36
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.150
This is what I think.

37
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.410
You two see the same event very differently.

38
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.870
Does this happen often with you two?

39
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.010
Yes.

40
00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:07.230
No.

41
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:08.630
Yes.

42
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.210
What are you talking about?

43
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:11.710
Okay.

44
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:12.850
This is good.

45
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:13.730
This is good.

46
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.870
It's good to see things differently.

47
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.490
I have an idea.

48
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.670
We will call the show

49
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.410
He Said, She Said.

50
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.930
For every story, you tell a different point of view.

51
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.410
That is a great idea, Kelly.

52
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.670
Pete, we are different.

53
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.550
That's why I thought of you for this job.

54
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.030
I think you two understand perfectly.

55
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.370
Let's get to work.
